g the middle of a protein  with with a with an epitope tag so for  us it all has to do with splicing so  Maya Carden which is the only  transcription factor we've tagged to  date has four splice variants and those  splicing events as is most the case  occur at the end terminus so we wanted  to capture all the splice variants and  that's one of the reasons why we put it  at the c-terminus so that way we get all  four splice isoforms of my car that  wouldn't be the case if we went to the n  terminus but there's another reason the  N terminus has a very important  functional domain and we were worried  about disrupting that and so we did some  preliminary experiments in vitro where  we made an n-terminal versus a c  terminal flag tag or Mik tag mile Cardon  and then we just looked at its ability  to transactive eight reporters and what  we found was the c terminus was the best  place so for two reasons for splicing  and for for activity we put it at the c  terminus  very very important question and I think  before one dies in and just willy-nilly  celexa and versus see by a coin toss  there should be some due diligent  homework before making that important  decision all right so it looks like we  have time for just one more question  here and it's another kind of technical  design question and it's does the  location of the pants sequence matter it  sounds like the person asking this  question has found there are several  pants sequences in the region where  they're trying to edit one it's upstream  of the proto spacer the other is  downstream in general you do Jemmy  recommendations for how far away from  the Pam sequence you should do the edits  and if there's any directionality that  you see it's a really good question so  the person asking that question is well  aware of these design tools that will  show Pam sequences on the plus and minus  strand the key the the most important  thing is to get that Pam sequence as  close to for example the base that you  wish to 